Title: Run the full test suite on padme after each merge to main
Created: 2026-09-25
Author: HDMX-coding-agent

--- log ---
2026-09-25T07:45Z HDMX-coding-agent created — author asked whether moving the full suite from pre-merge to post-merge on padme would speed development; measured first, author said "Do it"
2026-09-25T10:30Z HDMX-coding-agent note streamlined with the 1180 suite at the author's request: same runner pattern as [[1141]] / [[1184]] (persistent checkout, cron, report surfaced in `/lair`), in its own checkout

--- body ---
## Context

The merge gate is `make check-fast` + `make lint` (~40 s). The full
`make check` runs before merging only when the diff touches the pipeline
surface, and `/lair` step 9 runs it on main after the fact, by hand. This
ticket makes that after-merge run automatic on padme and narrows what must
still run before a merge.

## Measurements (padme, main c32db6cf, 2026-09-25)

| Workers | Wall time |
|---|---|
| 4 (`make check` as it stood) | 4 min 33 s (CPU 41 min) |
| 8 | 3 min 31 s |
| 16 | 2 min 21 s |
| 24 | 2 min 34 s (load ~23: tests start their own processes) |

- 15 of the last 40 merges on main (2026-09-23 to 09-25) touched the pipeline
  surface; 11 were tickets only.
- The same failures at every worker count: the four country-migration tests
  of [[0940]] (real, on main) and a file-date artifact, below. No flake
  appeared with more workers.
- The worker count is now per machine: `PYTEST_WORKERS` in `.env` (PR adding
  it, same branch as this ticket); padme sets 16.
- Evidence that the after-merge run is where data-bound failures surface:
  [[0940]] was found by an ex-post full check, not before merging. The four
  tests need the pinned JETP documents, which doudou worktrees lack; whether
  they skip there has not been verified.

## Two traps for a runner in a fresh worktree

1. `make data` in a brand-new worktree on padme refuses: `dvc checkout`
   reports the JETP documents the checkout hook just placed as "unsaved
   files" and asks for `--force`. Cause not established.
2. After a forced `dvc checkout`, `test_refined_embeddings_freshness` and
   `test_refined_citations_freshness` fail: the files are written a few ms
   apart in an order the mtime comparison rejects. Main is not red; any fresh
   checkout is. The runner therefore uses one persistent checkout, not a new
   worktree per run.

## Design

- A persistent checkout on padme, separate from the author's primary
  checkout, that fast-forwards to `origin/main` and runs `make check` for each
  new merge commit (a user timer polling `origin/main` is enough; no forge
  webhook).
- A failure is reported against the merge commit that introduced it, with
  the failing node ids, and only once per failure (compare with the previous
  run's failure set). Reporting channel is the author's call: a ticket per
  new failure, as `/lair` step 9 does, or a notification.
- Once it runs, the merge gate in `AGENTS.md` narrows: the pre-merge full
  check stays only for diffs that write data or deposits (`dvc.yaml`, build
  and publish targets); other pipeline diffs go on `check-fast` + `lint`.
- `/lair` step 9 then reads the runner's results instead of running the suite.
- Same runner pattern as the collection passes of [[1141]] and [[1184]]
  (suite [[1180]]): reuse their wrapper and run-report format, but keep a
  separate checkout, so collection diffs never reach the test run.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The runner runs `make check` on padme for each new merge commit on main, in a persistent checkout, with `PYTEST_WORKERS=16`
- [ ] A new failure is reported once, naming the merge commit and the failing test ids
- [ ] The file-date artifact and the `make data` refusal do not affect the runner (persistent checkout, or fixed)
- [ ] `AGENTS.md` § Merge gate and `/lair` step 9 describe the new split
